 "title": "Sorting overview",
 "short": "Know the main sorts, and when sorted() is the answer.",
 "what": "Sorting puts items in order. Simple sorts compare neighbours and cost O(n²); smart ones split the work and cost O(n log n); counting sort skips comparisons when values are small whole numbers. In Python you almost always call sorted(), and you learn the others to explain trade-offs.",
 "analogy": "Tidying a bookshelf. Slide each new book into place (insertion), split the pile with a friend and combine (merge), pick a middle book and throw others left or right (quick), or use labelled bins by first letter (counting).",
